    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if we do a deload, are we talking just a week of light work or should we drop the total sets entirely? also, i'm worried about them losing momentum. how do we distinguish between actual CNS fatigue and just being mentally burnt out on the movement?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A proper deload typically lasts 48 to 72 hours rather than a full week, as this duration optimally reduces central nervous system fatigue while allowing metabolic byproducts to clear, though dropping total sets completely is also a valid strategy depending on their fatigue metrics. Distinguishing between CNS fatigue and mental burnout involves objectively measuring sets to failure and RPE scores over a few days, as objective performance deficits are the primary differentiator from simple lack of motivation. If they consistently hit lower RPEs with maintained form but fail to match previous recovery windows, it signals physiological fatigue that a short deload should readily resolve.",
